Rule 1 (expected_cardinality must be a non-negative integer)
{v_1 : int} |= v_1 ≥ 0
>>
Rule 3 (assert cardinality can be skipped if cardinality is already known and equal to the expected value)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : int} |= v_1 = v_2
>>
Rule 4 (assert cardinality can be skipped if expected value is negative one)
{v_1 : int} |= v_1 ≠ -1
>>
Rule 6 (cardinality can be zero only if the input dataset is empty)
{v_1 : int} |= if v_1 = 0 then true else v_1 > 0
>>
Rule 11 (The cardinality assertion should raise an error if the cardinality is different than expected)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : int} |= if v_1 ≠ v_2 then true else false
>>
Rule 12 (assert cardinality's expected value is between min and max of another tensor's data)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : tensor} |= min(v_2) ≤ v_1 ∧ v_1 ≤ max(v_2)
>>
Rule 13 (assert_cardinality is applicable if a condition holds true on a tensor)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : tensor} |= if ndim(v_2) > 0 then true else v_1 = 0
>>
Rule 14 (If the expected cardinality is a large number then it should be an integer type that can represent it.)
{v_1 : int} |= v_1 < 2147483647
>>
Rule 16 (Negative cardinality should be equal to the value of tf.data.experimental.UNKNOWN_CARDINALITY)
{v_1 : int} |= if v_1 < 0 then v_1 = -1
>>
Rule 17 (cardinality should be close to another estimated cardinality value.)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : int} |= (v_1 - v_2) < 100 ∧ (v_2 - v_1) < 100
>>
Rule 19 (If dataset is unbounded then do not assert a cardinality)
{v_1 : int} |= v_1 ≠ 0
>>
Rule 20 (assert cardinality is valid when the cardinality equals the length of list)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : list(int)} |= v_1 = v_2.len
>>
Rule 22 (assert cardinality is valid when the cardinality equals to shape of two tensors)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : tensor, v_3 : tensor} |= if shape(v_2,0) > shape(v_3,0) then v_1 = shape(v_2,0) else v_1=shape(v_3, 0)
>>
Rule 23 (assert cardinality is not None if dataset doesn't use bucket by sequence length)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : bool} |= if v_2 = false then v_1 ≠ -1
>>
Rule 25 (cardinality asserted must be smaller than total available RAM)
{v_1 : int} |= v_1 < 10000000000
>>
Rule 26 (assert cardinality is positive if samples are non-zero)
{v_1 : tensor, v_2 : int} |= if min(v_1) > 0 then v_2 > 0
>>
Rule 27 (Expected cardinality is smaller if the dataset is filtered)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : float} |= v_1 < v_2
>>
Rule 28 (Assert cardinality is valid if total number of samples are divisible by batch size)
{v_1: int, v_2: int} |= if v_2 > 0 then v_1 % v_2 = 0
>>
Rule 29 (Cardinality has to be a multiple of the number of devices if sharded)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : int} |= if v_2 > 0 then v_1 % v_2 = 0
>>
Rule 30 (The cardinality asserted must be positive if there is no filtering based on a condition)
{v_1: int, v_2: bool} |= if v_2 = false then v_1 > 0
>>
Rule 31 (Cardinality shouldn't be defined if the number of epochs are infinite)
{v_1: int, v_2: int} |= if v_2 = -1 then v_1 = -1
>>
Rule 32 (assert cardinality is one if you take only one element)
{v_1: int} |= v_1 = 1
>>
Rule 33 (Assert cardinality must be equal to the product of the tensor's shape when flattening)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : tensor} |= if ndim(v_2) > 0 then v_1 = 1 ∧ ∀i ∈ [0, ndim(v_2) - 1] : v_1 = v_1 * shape(v_2, i) else v_1 = 0
>>
Rule 34 (If we zip two datasets, the new cardinality must be the smallest of the two)
{v_1: int, v_2: int} |= v_1 ≤ v_2
>>
Rule 35 (Expected cardinality must be smaller than number of examples present in TFRecord)
{v_1: int} |= v_1 < 2000000
>>
Rule 37 (If dataset is repeated by a factor of n, new cardinality is old cardinality * n)
{v_1: int, v_2: int} |= v_1 ≥ 0 ∧ v_2 ≥ 0
>>
Rule 38 (if we apply a take transformation, cardinality must be smaller)
{v_1: int, v_2: int} |= v_1 <= v_2
>>
Rule 39 (If a dataset is shard, its cardinality should be unknown)
{v_1: bool, v_2: int} |= if v_1 = true then v_2 = -1
>>
Rule 42 (After batching, cardinality is ceiling(old_cardinality / batch_size))
{v_1: int, v_2: int} |= if v_2 > 0 then true else v_1 = 0
>>
Rule 43 (When using padded batch, the number of element must be same than first dimension of tensor.)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : tensor} |= v_1 = shape(v_2, 0)
>>
Rule 44 (If cardinality is larger than any number, the system will be saturated of memory)
{v_1: int} |= v_1 < 100000000
>>
Rule 45 (Assert cardinality value is always same if is cache previously)
{v_1: int, v_2: bool} |= if v_2 = true then true else v_1>0
>>
Rule 47 (If expected cardinality is unknown then we shouldn't try to determine its properties)
{v_1 : int} |= if v_1 = -1 then true else v_1 > 0
>>
Rule 48 (If file exists cardinality must be a positive value.)
{v_1: int, v_2: bool} |= if v_2 = true then v_1 > 0
>>
Rule 49 (Cardinality must be at most batch size if drop_remainder is true.)
{v_1: int, v_2: int, v_3 : bool} |= if v_3 = true then v_1 <= v_2
>>
Rule 50 (Cardinality is less than window size if sliding through window method is used)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : int} |= v_1 < v_2
>>
Rule 51 (If cardinality is unknown then length of tensor must be unknown as well)
{v_1: int, v_2: tensor} |= if v_1 = -1 then true else ndim(v_2)>0
>>
Rule 52 (If there are not elements to be added the cardinality value should be at least one.)
{v_1 : int} |= v_1 >= 1
>>
Rule 53 (asserting cardinality with another known cardinality, the number of element on the intersection.)
{v_1 : int, v_2: int} |= v_1 = v_2
>>
Rule 54 (after applying `concatenate` the number of elements must be higher)
{v_1: int, v_2: int} |= v_1 > v_2
>>
Rule 55 (Asserted cardinality it is close to the mean cardinality of all samples.)
{v_1 : int, v_2 : tensor} |= (v_1 - min(v_2)) > 0 ∧ (max(v_2) - v_1) > 0
>>
Rule 57 (Asserting cardinality after applying filter transformation.)
{v_1: int, v_2: tensor} |= v_1 <= shape(v_2, 0)
>>
Rule 58 (If data is of type string, then cardinality should be length of string)
{v_1: str, v_2: int} |= v_2 = v_1.len
